Result:
The Dockerized Oxia real-service script passed. JUnit XML reports
`TargetQuotaGrantStoreTest.realOxiaOwnerTakeoverReopensTargetRootAndCompletesCloseGc`: 1 test,
0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skipped.

The first Owner acquires an ACQUIRING lease and commits the initial Target root under that exact lease guard; the Store
Owner epoch remains unchanged until `TargetWorkerOwnerActivation.activate` persists it and CASes the lease to ACTIVE.
The replacement Owner is also acquired as ACQUIRING, rejects the stale old Owner read without a Store write, reopens
the root under a guarded read, and activates from the previous Store epoch. Exact retry with the original ACQUIRING
lease returns the same ACTIVE identity before composing the source runtime and Worker; bounded Close GC then completes.
Before the old Store closes, the test releases its real ACTIVE Oxia lease while that Worker has a Close-GC task
queued. Running the pending task returns FAILED, marks the old Target source runtime fenced, leaves the RocksDB sequence
unchanged, and preserves the OPEN Close cursor. The Oxia lease backend and RocksDB Store are real services; assignment,
SourcePosition, Control registration, StartAuthority and source consumer are test fixtures. This verifies live Owner loss
on the queued maintenance path, but not source apply/ACK fencing, same-Target sibling isolation, Kafka/Pulsar source
fencing, production assignment/Start/Commit providers, or application Host bootstrap. Other optional real-service smoke tests in the
script were skipped because their separate service inputs were not configured.
